.TH rm 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
rm \- remove files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rm
.I "file ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rm
unlinks each
.IR file .
A file whose last name is removed goes away; a file that still has
another link, or that a running process still holds open, does not.
.SH OPTIONS
None.
.SH NOTES
This is the important one.
.B rm
does not merely lack
.BR \-r ,
.B \-f
and
.BR \-i :
it
.I silently ignores
any argument that begins with a
.BR \- .
.PP
.RB `` "rm \-rf junk" ''
therefore removes the file called
.B junk
and says nothing about
.BR \-rf .
.RB `` "rm \-rf" ''
on its own does nothing at all and exits 0.  Nothing warns you.
.PP
To remove a directory tree you must empty it yourself, deepest first,
and then
.BR rmdir (1)
the directories \- or use
.BR find (1)
to drive
.BR rm .
.PP
A file that cannot be removed is reported, but the exit status is still
0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rmdir (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR cp (1)
